System Administration <dslayer@dragonslair.net> wrote in message
news:38FF738C.B9DC2AE8@dragonslair.net...
>Tom I counted the chips per simm as you suggested and they all have 12 per
simm. Now one >thing that i do notice is that they (4 total) are all
different. Do you happen to know if >Alpha processors are super picky about
things such as this? I would imagine they would >prefer that all 4 are all
the same, am I correct in my thinking here?
>Thanks again in advance for your input ; )

You are very correct. Long ago I worked on a 486 computer, running WfW
(yes, it was *very* ancient :-) ) On that machine, the CDROM-drive
functioned
properly in MSDOS, but acted rather weird in WfW. Of course, the first
thing we did was blame it on Wfw. It turned out that someone put some
memory in the machine, with different SIMMs. The effect was that MSDOS
functioned properly, being confined to the memory bank with the identical
SIMMs, but WfW, which used the faulty bank as well, did not.

The reason for this behaviour is the following: A CPU (be it x386, Alpha or
something else, it does not matter) has a certain data bus width, e.g. 128
bit
for Alpha. One SIMM has a width of 32 bits. Therefore, you require four
SIMMs
to access the full width of the CPU's data bus. In comparison, my own system
uses DIMMs with a width of 64 bits, hence you require two to cover the data
bus. Thus, in one memory bank must be four SIMMs (or two DIMMs).
Now, how is this data bus used? Regardless of the size of the data being
transferred, always the whole bus is accessed. While the system is usually
capable of handling slightly different memory timings in different memory
parts, it cannot handle different timings on different parts of the bus.
All data bits in one access should arrive at the same time, not some of
them later than others.

So the conclusion is clear. Make sure that all SIMMs or DIMMs in a memory
bank are identical, not only the same brand but also the same type. Even
different memory modules from the same manufacturer can cause trouble.
SIMMs/DIMMs in different memory banks need not be the same.


--
Martin Sinot
Nijmegen, Netherlands
martin@spase.nl





