X-Accept-Language: en
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=EUC-KR
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha,comp.periphs.scsi

I thought that any PCI scsi controller host-adapter boards would
work as long as they are bootable from Intel/Linux.  I seem to
be realizing that that is not really the case.

Even a Centos CI-4500 UW scsi controller which has
LSI (Symbios in the past) chip 53c875 cannot be used to boot
an LX system, while Diamond Fireport 40UW with the
same chip can be used to boot the system.

A similar situation happens with the UP2000 system.
Both Symbios-based controller cards cannot
boot the system, while a Qlogic (with its own chipset) can boot it.

Why is it that way?

Does an Alpha system have a SROM or PROM entry that is used
to recognize the attached PCI scsi controllers of various
manufacturers?

Best regards,

G. Hugh Song

