In article <8e54sv$jt0$1@nntp6.u.washington.edu>,
  kargl@troutmask.apl.washington.edu (Steven G. Kargl) wrote:
> In article <8e50ue$vu8$1@nnrp1.deja.com>,
> 	Leon Muratov <lmuratov@my-deja.com> writes:
> > In article <3905E6E5.B4E2DFF5@moene.indiv.nluug.nl>,
> >
> > So any ideas what can cause 50% slowdown on Linux?
>
> Memory management and I/O.
>
> As already mentioned Tru64 has page coloring while linux
> doesn't.  This can affect memory management which your
> "benchmark" number suggest is the problem.  Does your
> program allocate/deallocate a large number of small
> arrays?  This could lead to misalignment and fragmentation
> which may causes inefficient use of the cache.
>
> >
> > And what can I do to figure it out before I erase Linux disk?
> >
>
> Use the profiler to find the bottleneck(s) in the code.  You
> need to add the -pg flag to the compilation and use gprof
> to see how the code uses the cpu.  Of course, this suggestion
> is only valid if Compaq provides this with their linux Fortran
> compiler.
>
> --
> Steve
>
Steve, thank you,  this is a good point ! I had to think about it myself
first, since I already
so a similar problem on HP.
However, I was under impression that cache management is done mainly by
hardware.
 And if compilers are the same (as COMPAQ fortran group stated)
executables and memory layouts should be similar.  So what is so bad for
Linux that was OK for Tru64?
What should I avoid?  What is an efficient way of handling memory in
Linux?

The program was originally written for Cray (not by me). It handle the
memory as following
In the beginning it allocate a large chunk of memory and create an
internal "heap" from which it supplies memory to subroutines.  Most of
allocations are semi-permanent
(for long time) but there are some for small arrays. I understand that
this prevents compiler from any memory optimization, but to change a
structure of the program with 25K lines brrr... I tried once and quit.
This probably has to be done anyway, but first I would like to get
answers to questions above.

Speaking about libraries:
We are not using any special libraries exept base runtime, all special
functions are computed inside (result of porting this program to several
different platforms).  Program does not have too many Exp, Sin and Cos
it mostly does additions, multiplications,. and some Sqrts. And if it
was a libproblems
benchmarks ratio most ikely (parts of program could scale differently
with N)  would be the similar for small and large program.  99.9% that
this is a memory problem.

Best,
Leon

BTW when I checked few LAPACK routines compiled from source by COMPAQ
compilers they run as fast as their twins from extended math libraries.


