Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha


I've installed a beta version of SuSE 6.3 on a 1000A 5/333, which
ought to be the same.

I was able to boot directly from the CD-ROM from the SRM console
with boot dka400 -fi etc/generic.gz -fl "root=/dev/scd0"

(I think it was generic.gz: if not it's vmlilnuz.gz)

I was given a lot of help from our Linux people on this, and there
are some problems you have to watch out for.

You have to ask to partition the disk, then to Alt F2 to get to
the prompt and FDISK to partition the disk.  Leave cylinder 1 and
maybe 2 blank, then create a swap partition as a, and a big
root partition as b.  Cylinder 1 must be blank because the boot
driver will go there.  Use BSD type labels.

Contrary to some other installation instructions and previous
versions of SuSE, if you want to be able to hardware boot the
hard disk directly, the boot partition must be the second partition
(b) on the disk.  It should be ext2.

Once you do this you halt and reboot the CD-ROM.  Select
partitioning again, and when it comes up and you see the a and
b partitions on your disk, select F5 "Expert Mode".  You
must change the inode count for the b (root ext2) partition to be
1024.  By default it will probably be larger, and this again
will make it impossible to hardware boot the disk.

After this, I found that the installation went quite well.
Just answer the questions, select the packages you want, and
let it go.

After the system is installed and booted from the hard disk, you
have to do some cleanup.  If the disk you installed on isn't the
first drive on the SCSI bus (i.e., dka0) you will have to edit
/etc/aboot.conf to set up the right boot.  Actually, you may have
to do this anyway to set the boot options.  My system disk is dka200,
so the first line of my aboot.conf looks like this:

0:2/boot/vmlinuz.gz ro root=/dev/sdc2 bootdevice=/dev/sdc2

Make sure aboot.conf also has a single dash "-" in the first column
of the last line.  This seems to be important when the file is
read, otherwise there is an overrun.

You also have to execute the swriteboot command to write the
bootstrap to the disk, as in:

swriteboot -v /dev/sdc /boot/bootlx


You might also want to look at the Compaq Linux web page, they
may have updated instructions there.

-- 
