bubble <j.j.b.weinberg@hccnet.nl> wrote in message
news:3908283E.B2CB22A4@hccnet.nl...
> Hi there,
>
> I was trying to compile blackbox using cxx from compaq.
> All was going well until the compiler hit Icon.cc where it gave the
> following error:
>
> cxx: Error: Icon.cc, line 137: identifier "strcmp" is undefined
> if (strcmp(name, "Unnamed")) {
> ____________^
>
> could comeone xplain why the identifier is undefined,
> and strcmp is a function or am I really mistaken.
> Keep in mind I am a novice at C.
>
> Thanks for your trouble
>
> Jigal

#include <string.h>

In C, it is allowed to use a function without declaring it before.
The compiler will issue a warning and treat it as a function with
unknown parameters and returning int. Very dangerous on Alphas,
if the function happens to return a pointer.
In C++ this is forbidden. Functions must always be declared before
use. g++ allows the use of undefined functions on the same basis
as C, but this is not correct (g++ allows too much). Most other
C++ compilers correctly reject undeclared functions.
Unfortunately, some programmers are sloppy, use undeclared functions
and turn off compiler warnings rather than fixing them. Reason why
a program can be accepted by g++ and not by another compiler (like
Compaq cxx).


