> LInux:       fort -r8 -fast -align dcommons  bnd.f
> Tru64 :     f77  -r8 -O bnd.f
> 
> I know about "page coloring" on Linux (Can somebody explain me what
> exactly does it mean - I can only guess) But based on what I heard
> before, I expected 10-15% drop not 50%!

Have to ask someone else about that.

> Could it be something else that I missed?

Performance measurement is *NOT* as trivial as many people (including
some, but by no means all of the ones on this newsgroup) portray it.
Many things can affect performance and to different degrees on different
codes.  That's why testing with an actual application program (as you
appear to be doing) is always desirable.  It is not at all unusual
to find that performance differences that show up as 10-15% on
one application or benchmark might show up as 50% differences on
another.

But, of course, it is worthwhile to try to understand why you are
seeing a performance difference.  After all, it might turn out
to be something fixable.

One big thing that you missed is mentioning what compilers you are
talking about (including version numbers).  I can't tell just from the
command lines.

You mentioned size of the application, but not of the
computer.  If either system is swapping to disk, then
many performance issues come into play (and buying more
memory would likely be a better solution than trying to
tune the swapping - certainly for the sizes you are quoting,
all of which are modest for current momory prices).

-- 
Richard Maine
maine@altair.dfrc.nasa.gov



