The answer to that would be the German "Jein" (a word made up from
combining "Ja" [yes] and "Nein" [no]).

If you want it, you can have it. I won't "release" it, because it is

a) Not fully debugged --- I *know* it fails on a lot of stuff, but 
   I debugged it to the point where it worked for everything *I* used.
b) A bit specialized --- it won't currently take symbolic names for registers,
   and while I have a version which tries to handle FPU instructions, that
   handling certainly isn't up to scratch
c) REALLY REALLY ugly code --- like so much code that started out as a quick
   hack to solve a problem, and then grew, and grew, and grew some more,
   without ever being designed
d) Limited to EV5 scheduling
e) Suboptimal --- it does a pretty good job at scheduling, but it sometimes
   gets into a bit of a tangle due to the "thou shalt read thy memory one
   cycle or three cycles after writing, but not two, lest thou shalt suffer
   a heavy penalty" kind of rules, and produces code that takes longer to
   execute than it should.

Now that I have thoroughly deterred you ;-), let me say this: If anybody
wants to hammer the whole thing into some presentable form (or even just
take it as a starting point, replacing each and every single line in what
amounts to a though-through rewrite) --- you are welcome! I just have
neither time nor motivation to actually do it properly right now.....

Bernie
