I've been using the .config which follows my signature to build bootable
kernels on RH 6.2 (2.2.14).  It came from Puget Sound Data Systems along
with our DS10s, and was modified slightly by me.  Actually, this
configuration is for a network boot, for a regular boot you can omit the
root NFS and BOOTP support.  To use it do: 

(place the file as /usr/src/linux/.config )
% cd /usr/src/linux
% make dep
% make clean
% make boot
% make bootpfile

I had to make a variety of other changes to files in a regular RH 6.2
install to get it to boot over the network properly, but this kernel config
is a start. 

Regards,

David Mathog
mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu
Manager, sequence analysis facility, biology division, Caltech 
