On Wed, 26 Apr 2000 14:35:01 GMT, Leon Muratov <lmuratov@my-deja.com>
wrote:

> And if compilers are the same (as COMPAQ fortran group stated)

I'm not aware that anyone from the Compaq Fortran group suggested any
such thing.

You said that the compilers you are using are:

> Compilers: UNIX DIGITAL Fortran 77 V5.1-156 (old)
> 	 LINUX (the latest Feb01 COMPAQ) from  cfal-1.0-7.alpha.rpm

These are certainly not the same compiler - the Linux compiler is
Fortran 95 and is some two years newer.  However, assuming that you
are compiling the SAME source with these two compilers, in general I
would expect the Linux compiler to generate better code, since it has
a code generator/optimizer several generations newer than the older
F77 compiler you are using.

Our own tests suggest that the performance advantage of Tru64 UNIX
over Linux that you are seeing is, while a bit more than we typically
see, not really out of line for some applications.  We typically see
20-35% for large applications.  I do think that the suggestion to
build a statically linked image on Tru64 and running it as a test on
Linux will produce useful data. (I am obligated to note that,
technically, doing this violates the terms of your Tru64 UNIX license
agreement, since it means you will be running OSF-licensed code (from
the Tru64 UNIX libraries) on a system not covered by a license from
OSF.)

Someone else mentioned profile-directed feedback and other
optimization tools.  I assume that you did not make use of these in
your runs on Tru64 UNIX - you have to explicitly ask for them.


