In article <38FD01E5.B608D137@ix.netcom.com>, Greg Folkert <gfolkert@ix.netcom.com> writes:
>Of course I still can't get my stinking ev4/233 2100 A550MP to even boot Linux
>right now.
>

Page 15 of the Alpha/Sparc installation guide for RH 6.2 shows that as an
"unsupported system", and the 2100a as "currently incompatible".  Is this
for business use or just messing around?  If the latter, you can get VMS
for it from DECUS for free, and Tru64 from Compaq for around $100. I
believe that NetBSD will run on it as well (with no usage restrictions). 
(Or it would make quite an interesting end table...)

Other things to consider:

1. If it's multiprocessor pull all but one and see if will boot.
2. If you've got a PC around you can temporarily strip for parts put in a
   RH supported graphics card and SCSI card and try it again.   Could be
   that there's some oddness in the SCSI card or Graphics card support.
   For instance, DEC having custom ROM in them that tell the CPU they are
   something Linux doesn't recognize, even though they are really standard
   NCR, S3 type parts.
3. Try booting it diskless over the net from another Linux Alpha.  That 
   should get you into Kudzu which hopefully will tell you something useful
   like "what is that SCSI card?"  If it does that you may be able to 
   kludge up a working system by copying and renaming a driver to match,
   for instance, the NCR scsi driver even though the SCSI card says it's
   a "DEC so and so".

Good luck,

David Mathog
