General info:

Unaligned traps happen when the data in memory is arranged
for one type of architecture (like x86) and is ported to 
some other architecture with different memory arrangement
rules (like Alpha).

An example:  On intel 'long' data type is 4 bytes, on Alpha,
'long' is 8 bytes.  Alphas expect that all long's will be
found at memory addresses that are a multiple of 8.  Since
8 is the size of pointers and longs on Alphas, all return
values (addresses) from memory allocators are multiples of
at least 8, but some older programs just assumed that their
data would be aligned for 4 byte pointers.

So, when a 'long' is located at a multiple of 4, and not 8
bytes in memory, the kernel notices this and complains by
issuing the unaligned trap.  This does not really affect
the behavior of the program, it just makes it run lots
slower.  It can indicate serious problems with the program
if the misaligned data is a pointer and half of it is being
overwritten with other data, which can happen with some
very badly written programs.  Don't bet on it, I really
doubt that anything that bad would even run long enough to
be released.

The solution is typically to fix and recompile the
applications with no assumptions about the size of data 
types.  This can be a bit of work.  I'd probably recommend
that you upgrade to the latest release of RedHat, since
other people have probably fixed the programs by now.

