> Above paragraph seems to end half way through a sentence?

Hi,

Apologies, but I did threaten to splurge... I'll *try* and write
in something approaching English this time!

The external cache is direct mapped and physically addressed
(i.e. *physical* address modulo cache size gives location
in the external cache ). 

The problem is that laying out the data sensibly in
virtual page numbering gives no guarantee that the physical
translation is sensibly laid out. Because the external cache
is direct mapped it is possible to read two streams for which
data mapping to the same locations in external cache for accesses
with short temporal separation. This can cause references to one stream
to evict already loaded data which will be needed soon.
Note if the Ext cache was 2 or 4 way associative this thrashing wouldn't
occur (or at least be as bad) because the eviction would be implemented
on an oldest of 2/4 references being evicted policy.

Page coloring is a way to detect this combined with dynamic relocation
of the cuplrit virtual pages in the physical memory space to prevent
such conflicts in future.

