Jason:

I too got one of those freebies, I've had great luck with mine.
You'll need RAM, 72 pin TRUE Parity simms, at least 32 mb, but more is
better.  Your Multia will support up to 128mb.  If you have time to burn
you can sometimes find memory at EBay, HitechCafe, or Computer Geeks, but
if you don't want to screw around just call Don at Hess Electronics (714)
664-9838.  He's got everything you might need and at great prices.
An external Hard disk and CD is a must as these boxes develop a lot of
heat, drives last longer outside.  If your system does not have an
external SCSI-2 connector, you can use the drive cables from a VAX3100
M76. It has a weird but useful male scsi connector that will go on to the
single scsi connector inside your box, and you can thread it out the back
to the external box.  It's not very pretty, but it works.
Floppy drive is a real plus, especially if you plan to install Red Hat
Linux.

Software, the Multia runs Windoze NT (3.5 & 4.0), Red Hat Linux, NetBSD
Linux, Some flavors of UNIX, and Open VMS.


Here are some useful and educational links:

http://home.earthlink.net/~djesys/vms/hobbyist/multia.html
http://www.netbsd.org/Ports/alpha/multiafaq.html
http://www.brouhaha.com/~eric/computers/udb.html
http://www.openvms.digital.com/freeware/multia/
http://www.progsoc.uts.edu.au/~telford/reference/multia/
http://ftp.dreamtime.org/pub/multia/
http://www.lx040-001.gsbme.unsw.edu.au/~shaunc/multia/multia-users/

That should be enough to keep you awake late at night.

Good luck!!

Tom Crabtree

