Abraham Theodorus Stolk <bram.s@chello.nl> wrote:
> And cxx has problems finding stdlib.h.

        I have found that the Compaq C++ compiler *does* work on
Debian 2.2 (potato).  What you have to do is to update the config
files for the compilers (both C and C++) with the scripts provided,
called create-comp-config.sh which are in
/usr/lib/compaq/{cxx-6.3.6.9,ccc-6.2.9.002-2}/alpha-linux/bin.  Once
you get that done you're set.  Note that you will definitely need the
Compaq binutils to link, even the latest binutils from Debian won't
work (the linker falls over).

        I'm finding that porting to the new compiler will be a lot of
work: lots of extra "std::" qualifiers to add (I'm using -std
strict_ansi since using -std gnu breaks the qualified names I'm
already using [error message is 'namespace "std" has no member
"blah"'].  It also seems very picky about pointers to functions as
arguments to constructors etc.  It should be great when I get it all
to work, though.  My previous experiments with the C compiler on
RedHat/Alpha showed that gcc generate lousy, slow code in comparison.



--
James Macnicol
j-macnicol@adfa.edu.au



