Organization: Arbeitsgruppe Medizinische Physik
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha


I would be interested too. At least there exist an object oriented
numerics mailinglist at http://oonumerics.org/oon/, some people do C++
and some Fortran and some even Python.

Note that there is an O'Reilly Book about HPC, but it isn't precisely
new and doesn't covers Alpha details very much. However it maybe good
for understanding principles. 

What would be interesting to me is, for example what is the exact
overhead of thread context switches and of shared data synchronisation
on dual SMP systems ? What one needs is either a lot of experiments or
some specific data. I tried some sort of double-threaded convolution
and got some awful results which seemed to mean that thread
synchronization in some circumstances could cost as much as some ten
thousend multiplications!


As the Alpha 21364 will support multithreading, the whole SMP issue
would be interesting to many more people. Also one has to note that
performance depends a lot upon the implementation of the alpha
architecture, so one should try to find a way to be not overly
implementation-dependant.


Johannes



